<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Syria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Syrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Syria is a historical region of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, lying between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Its cities, trade routes and fertile lands made it one of the most contested regions in history, fought over by the Hittite, Egyptian, Assyrian, Seleucid, Ptolemaic, Roman and Byzantine powers. In the Islamic period it was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and later a core territory of the Ayyubid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> states.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/syria.jpg" alt="Syria" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Modification by AnonMoos of PD image File:Flag of Syria (1930–1958, 1961–1963).svg (previous non-vector versions were by Coup de … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Syria_(2025-).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Perhaps 100,000 Arabs then lived in Ifriqiya, most of them immigrants from <b>Syria</b> and Iraq, but the Berbers remained the great majority.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He then extended his campaigns into Elam to the east and up the Euphrates into <b>Syria</b>, reaching the Cedar Forest and the Silver Mountains towards the Mediterranean, while later legend carried his armies into Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Antigonus I Monophthalmus, ‘the One-Eyed’, one of Alexander the Great's senior officers, had governed Phrygia since 333 BCE, and in the wars of the Successors after 323 BCE he built up the largest of the competing realms, stretching from western Asia Minor to <b>Syria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nicephorus II Phocas retook Cilicia from the Arabs in 965 the Byzantines settled Christians from <b>Syria</b> and Armenia there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He took Damascus that autumn, progressively absorbed the Zengid principalities of <b>Syria</b> and northern Mesopotamia through a combination of military pressure and diplomacy, sent his brother Turanshah to conquer Yemen in 1174, securing the Red Sea route, took Aleppo in 1183 and received the submission of Mosul in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Basil II (r. 976–1025) used these resources to conquer Bulgaria after decades of war, earning the epithet Bulgar-slayer, and left the empire stretching from the Danube to northern <b>Syria</b> — its greatest extent since the seventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
